  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. I read both back from Kibana afterwards to confirm they exist.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request, with:\n- **Request details:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `response`, `bytes`.\n- **Client details:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`.\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`.\n\nThere are about 14,000 requests in total, split by response code as:\n\n| Response code | Requests |\n|---|---|\n| 200 | 12,832 |\n| 404 | 801 |\n| 503 | 441 |\n\nThe only 5xx code in the data is 503. The timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so part of the data is dated in the future.\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-2e584df5 Web logs overview\"** ([open](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-2e584df5-web-logs-overview-0bccd413f641)). It defaults to the last 7 days and has seven panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time by response code (bar chart)\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and average bytes\n- Top 10 source countries\n\nI haven't opened the dashboard in a browser, so I haven't seen the panels draw.\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-2e584df5 5xx spike\"** (id `5c8a9059-7a95-4990-b9fc-2618070b3fb6`).\n- It runs every minute and searches `kibana_sample_data_logs` for responses from 500 to 599.\n- It fires when more than 10 of those requests arrive within 5 minutes, based on `timestamp`.\n- Its status is `ok`. Run on its own, the same filter matches the 441 503 responses in the index.\n- No notification action is attached, so when it fires it records an alert in Kibana but sends no message. To get notified, it needs a connector such as email or Slack added.",
